
  [1mThe fork:[0m today [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m is pure — it takes a [38;5;153m{username, password}[39m object and returns a result — while the submit
  listener separately does the DOM work (reading [38;5;153m#username[39m/[38;5;153m#password[39m by id, and reporting via [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m). Reuse
  could mean sharing only the pure rule-checking, or also sharing the repetitive wiring every form otherwise rewrites.
  This decides how much a second form has to write by hand, and it's cheap to widen later but awkward to narrow once
  forms depend on the wiring.

  - [1mPure validation only[0m — module takes a data object and rules, returns errors. Each form keeps its own listener, field
    reading, and error display. Smallest interface, trivially testable, no DOM assumptions. Cost: every new form
    re-writes the same ~10 lines of boilerplate, which is most of what's actually duplicated.
  - [1mPure validation + a form binder[0m — the above, plus one helper that attaches to a [38;5;153m<form>[39m, collects field values, runs
    the rules, and hands you the result. A second form becomes a rule declaration plus one call. Cost: the module now
    makes DOM assumptions (how fields are named, when validation fires), and needs a jsdom-style setup to test that
    half.
  - [1mFull solution incl. error rendering[0m — also injects error messages into the DOM. Cost: forces markup conventions on
    every form and bakes in styling decisions you haven't made. This is the YAGNI casualty given there's no second form
    yet.

  [1mMy recommendation: pure validation only.[0m You said this is preparatory with no concrete second form — and a binder's
  design depends heavily on what those forms actually look like (when errors show, how fields are identified). Extract
  the pure core now; add the binder when the second form tells you what it needs.
